WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.540
Creating a child process from privileged C code is a high-value design decision because the child may inherit more than the developer intended.

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:20.920
Identity, environment variables, current working directory, open descriptors, handles, resource limits, signal behavior, console state, and configuration context can all travel from parent to child.

3
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.940
The parent should decide what is passed, not leave it to defaults.

4
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.460
Program selection should be explicit.

5
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:33.960
Use trusted absolute paths or platform-appropriate lookup rules that are documented and reviewed.

6
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:40.300
Arguments should be structured values controlled by the program, not fragments assembled from ambiguous text.

7
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:48.960
Where the platform offers a direct process creation API, avoid unnecessary shell interpretation for operations that can be represented directly.

8
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.760
The environment and working directory should be set deliberately.

9
00:00:52.860 --> 00:01:03.820
A child process that inherits a broad environment from a privileged parent may receive configuration, paths, diagnostics, or credentials that are not needed for its task.

10
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:10.160
A controlled environment and known working directory make child behavior easier to test and reason about.

11
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.800
Descriptor or handle inheritance should be minimized.

12
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:20.300
The child should receive only the files, sockets, pipes, or handles it needs.

13
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:27.440
Mark unrelated resources as not inheritable or close them before the child starts, depending on the platform and design.

14
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.860
This reduces accidental access and makes resource ownership clearer.

15
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:40.060
A good process-launch design also includes identity, limits, status, timeouts, and cleanup.

16
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:49.520
Decide whether the child should run with reduced authority, what resource limits apply, how long it may run, how exit status is handled, and how partial work is cleaned up.

17
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:55.860
The parent should log outcomes in a way that supports operations without exposing sensitive command data or configuration.
